esp32: keep CCOUNT and the tick timers right when the CPU clock changes

Arduino core builds panicked within seconds on the fork in about half of
runs: as "Cache error" (an interrupt that ESP-IDF reports as one), or
with Espressif's interrupt matrix as "Interrupt wdt timeout". Checked
with GDB at the panic: core 0's CCOUNT was ~235M cycles past CCOMPARE0
with no timer interrupt pending, so its FreeRTOS tick had stopped.

Arduino switches the CPU to 240 MHz at run time (setCpuFrequencyMhz in
initArduino), while the tick timers are armed; ESP-IDF firmware switches
during early boot, which is why it never showed this. The machine set
each CPU's input clock directly, so the CPU never learnt of the change:
CCOUNT was rescaled and a pending CCOMPARE deadline could end up a whole
counter wrap away (~18 s at 240 MHz).

This takes Espressif's fix: xtensa_cpu_clock_changed() rebases CCOUNT
before a rate change and moves pending CCOMPARE deadlines after it,
registered as the CPU's clk-in callback; and the esp32-picsimlab machine
drives both CPUs from one cpu-clk source clock, as Espressif's esp32.c
does, since a clock's callback only runs when its source propagates.

Before: Blink panicked in 6 to 9 of 12 runs of 12 s. After: 16 of 16
clean. Swapping in
Espressif's interrupt matrix or timer group was tried and is not needed.

target/xtensa parts taken from espressif/qemu, tag
esp-develop-9.2.2-20261109 (7411393a2f61e6f0b05af00276d30780769e0839):
target/xtensa/op_helper.c, cpu.c, cpu.h; the machine change follows
their hw/xtensa/esp32.c. QEMU and Espressif Systems (Shanghai) Co. Ltd.
